Here is a third patient case scenario, focused on a common gastrointestinal adverse reaction, ideal for your Task 1 analysis and video recording:

Patient Details

Patient: 28-year-old female

Chief Complaint: Severe epigastric pain, burning sensation in the upper abdomen, and dark, tarry stools (melena).

Relevant Medical History: Chronic migraine; no history of peptic ulcer disease, H. pylori infection, or alcohol use.

Timeline & Medication History

Day 1: Patient experienced a severe, multi-day migraine episode. Started self-administering Ibuprofen 400 mg every 6 hours (total 1600 mg/day).

Day 5: Increased dosage to Ibuprofen 800 mg three times daily (2400 mg/day) due to persistent pain.

Day 10: Began experiencing sharp upper stomach pain and nausea, which worsened after eating.

Day 12: Noticed black, tarry stools and mild dizziness. Presented to urgent care.

Intervention: Endoscopy revealed acute gastric mucosal erosions. Ibuprofen was immediately discontinued, and a Proton Pump Inhibitor (Omeprazole) was prescribed.

Follow-up (1 Week): Abdominal pain completely subsided, melena resolved, and hemoglobin stabilized.

Case Analysis Breakdown

Suspected Reaction: Acute NSAID-Induced Gastric Ulcer / Gastrointestinal Bleeding.

Suspected Drug: Ibuprofen.

Causality Rationale:

Pharmacological Mechanism: High-dose, continuous NSAID usage inhibits COX-1 enzymes, severely reducing protective gastric mucosal prostaglandins and leading to mucosal injury and erosion.

Temporal Relationship: Symptoms developed progressively over 12 days of high-dose NSAID exposure.

De-challenge: Immediate cessation of Ibuprofen alongside acid-suppressive therapy led to rapid symptom resolution.

Alternative Causes: Patient has no prior history of GI disorders, non-smoker, and no concurrent gastrotoxic medications.
